%n puršumum | ša tāmuru || ilka | wīru(m)
#note: See @bib{RN117@244–245}: “Others have taken @i{wi-ru} as the god Wēr, though Renger and Bottéro drew attention to the problem such an interpretation posed with regard to the Yale tablet, where Wēr is the guardian of the Cedar Forest and thus an ally of Gilgameš’s enemy (OB III 131). Here, as elsewhere in the epic, Gilgameš’s god is not Wēr but Lugalbanda (see OB III 271, OB Harmal 15–16 and SB VI 165), and Wēr is thus quite out of place for this reason too. Accordingly one is led to look for a suitable adjective or common noun that can be written @i{wi-ru}. The existence of such a word can perhaps be supported by the lexical entry @i{Malku} I 52: @i{i-ru} = @i{ga-aš-ru} (@bib{temp_id_2401129561684543@425; also p. 435}, Explicit @i{Malku} I 143). This adjective probably also appears in the OB text about Narām-Sîn and Erra, where Erra is described as @akk{wi-ru-um ša-ka-al-mu-šu}, ‘mighty terror’ (@bib{RN2008a@361, 37; cf. note on p. 363}). The absence of mimation in our text is unusual, but note also @akk{da-an-nu} in rev. 6'.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "Schøyen₃" b+7'

%n ... | šunnû | pānūša
#note: See @bib{RN117@246}: “To my eyes the traces of the first word do not allow Renger’s @akk{nap-lu-uh₂-tum-ma}, ‘da war eine Schreckensgestalt’, though it cannot be discounted that some of the traces on my copy are remnants of poorly erased signs. I can offer no firm decipherment, only the comment that @akk{du!-lu!-uh₂-tum-ma}, ‘there was confusion’, is as likely as @i{napluḫtumma}, if not more so. One must also bear in mind that in the parallel line, rev. 2', the space suggests that <the> word is written more concisely, with three signs instead of four. The word @i{šunnû} could also be translated ‘double’, but here probably has implications of monstrousness (cf. Bottéro, ‘son aspect était monstreux’). The face of Anzû, the lion-headed eagle, was a by-word for horror, even the very image of death, as we know from Enkidu’s dream on his death-bed (MB Ur 65–6 // SB VII 168–9) and elsewhere (see the commentary on MB Ur 66).”
// L I.4 OB "Nippur" a+2

